  manages before rendering the new set, so stale messages can't survive a fix.

  [1mAccessibility, kept minimal.[0m Invalid inputs get [38;5;153maria-invalid="true"[39m (removed when they pass) and the error span gets
  [38;5;153mrole="alert"[39m. That's two attributes and it's the difference between an error a screen reader announces and one it
  doesn't — cheap enough to do now rather than retrofit.

  [1mError handling proper.[0m [38;5;153mattachValidation[39m is defensive about programmer error, not user error: it throws immediately if
  [38;5;153mformEl[39m is null or isn't a form, or if a schema key has no matching named input — a silent no-op there is the failure
  mode that wastes an afternoon. A rule that itself throws is not caught; that's a bug in the rule, and swallowing it
  would hide it. [38;5;153monValid[39m throwing is also left to propagate.

  [1mWhat this explicitly does not do,[0m per your earlier answers: no validation on blur or input (submit-time only), no
  submit-button disabling, no cross-field rules, no async rules. The schema shape leaves room for all of them later
  without changing the rules or the call signature.

  One open question — this repo has no linter, no formatter, and no test runner. Setting those up is cheapest right now,
  before there's code to retrofit, and this design is deliberately built around a pure testable core:

